+**Parameters:**
+*   **Learning Rate:** $4 \times 10^{-4}$ for AdamW [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Weight Decay:** 0.01 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Momentum:** $\beta_1=0.9, \beta_2=0.999$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scheduler:** OneCycleLR with 2,700 warm-up steps (9% of training), initial division factor 25, final division factor 100 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Training Steps:** 30,000 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Batch Size:** 32 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Loss:** $L = \text{MSE}(I_T, \hat{I}_T) + 0.05 \text{LPIPS}_{\text{VGG}}(I_T, \hat{I}_T)$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input Normalization:** RGB rescaled to [0, 255], then ImageNet normalized [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Decoder Channels:** 128 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Output Channels per Branch:** 37 (1 opacity, 2 offsets, 3 scale, 4 quaternion, 27 SH) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Padding:** Default 16 pixels [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Padding:** Without padding, empty target-border bands appear in rendered views; padding fills these newly exposed regions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Fixed Depth:** Fixed front-layer depth limits stereo-error correction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Teacher Artifacts:** Teacher artifacts can persist despite reconstruction-quality filtering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Descriptor:** Without the DPT descriptor, tabletop distortions and foreground-boundary artifacts occur [S1, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference is $O(HW)$ per pixel for the predictor and descriptor generation, dominated by the convolutional operations of the frozen encoder and the predictor $H$. It is significantly faster than per-scene optimization methods.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the descriptor $\Phi$ and the predictor weights. The Gaussian set size is $2 \times H \times W$ (front and back layers), which is large but manageable for typical resolutions (e.g., 256x256 or higher).
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   On StereoNVS: 21.261 PSNR, 0.9447 SSIM, 0.0818 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
-    *   On ReplicaGS: 30.266 PSNR, 0.9447 SSIM, 0.0818 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Composition improves LPIPS and SSIM but lowers PSNR by 0.614 dB on StereoNVS and improves by 0.237 dB on ReplicaGS compared to the composer-free variant [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance degrades without padding. Removing 16-pixel padding lowers PSNR by 3.168 dB on StereoNVS and 8.732 dB on ReplicaGS. Increasing padding to 64 pixels improves StereoNVS PSNR by 1.928 dB but lowers ReplicaGS PSNR by 0.101 dB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables nearby-view extrapolation from a single stereo pair; handles disocclusions via the back layer; handles outside-FoV content via canvas expansion; generalizes to unseen scenes.
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed front-layer depth limits stereo-error correction; teacher artifacts can persist; evaluation focuses on nearby views; large number of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a metric 3DGS representation is needed from a calibrated stereo pair for nearby-view synthesis, and per-scene optimization is prohibitive.
-*   **When not to use:** When large viewpoint changes are required, or when stereo depth accuracy is poor and cannot be corrected by the back layer.
+    *   **StereoNVS:** 21.261 PSNR, 0.9447 SSIM, 0.0818 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **ReplicaGS:** 30.266 PSNR, 0.9447 SSIM, 0.0818 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Enables nearby-view extrapolation without per-scene optimization [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Second Gaussian layer and expanded canvas provide capacity for disoccluded and outside-FoV content [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Improves LPIPS and SSIM with composition (though PSNR may drop slightly) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Fixed front-layer depth limits stereo-error correction [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Teacher artifacts can persist [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Evaluation focuses on nearby views; larger viewpoint changes are not fully evaluated [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When nearby-view extrapolation from a single calibrated stereo pair is required without per-scene optimization [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When large viewpoint changes are needed or when stereo-error correction is critical [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-StereoGaussians was introduced in 2026 (preprint) by authors associated with the SceneSplat-Stereo dataset and the FoundationStereo project [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of feed-forward 3DGS prediction (e.g., Charatan et al., 2024; Chen et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2025b) and adapts the residual attribute composition from SHARP (Mescheder et al., 2025) [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the gap in training data for stereo-based 3DGS by constructing SceneSplat-Stereo from quality-filtered 3DGS teachers [S1, unofficial]. It is used on StereoNVS and ReplicaGS datasets [S1, unofficial].
+StereoGaussians was introduced by Charatan et al. (2024) in the paper "StereoGaussians: Feed-Forward 3D Gaussian Splatting from Stereo Images" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of feed-forward 3DGS prediction, adapting it for stereo inputs by leveraging frozen pretrained stereo networks. The method was trained on the SceneSplat-Stereo dataset, which pairs stereo inputs with nearby target views from 803 training scenes [S1, unofficial]. It is used on StereoNVS and ReplicaGS datasets [S1, unofficial].
 
